  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Indonesian Employers Association (APINDO) produced a comprehensive assessment of needs and challenges to develop soft skills and developed policy proposals for implementation of Industry 4.0 strategies. 
Despite the introduction of the Omnibus Law on Job Creation (Law No.11/2020), soft skills development policies were treated in a fragmented way with the relevant responsibilities spread between three different ministries. 
To address this inefficiency and respond to the needs of member companies, APINDO conducted a study on &#xbf;gStrengthening Policy Advocacy towards Soft Skills Development in the Implementation of Industry 4.0&#xbf;h using information collected through surveys of 192 enterprises in 9 industrial sectors, 1 focus group discussion with 23 participants in November 2020 and 20 in-depth interviews. 
In November 2020, APINDO held a national seminar to discuss the policy direction for soft skills development with 167 participants from 9 industrial sectors. In March 2021, the recommendations for policy proposals were validated at a members&#xbf;f workshop and submitted to the government. APINDO&#xbf;fs policy proposal contributed to stimulating greater stakeholder collaboration towards developing demand-driven soft skills. APINDO demanded regulatory coherence between ministries. APINDO also recommended educational institutions to reform the curriculum and collaborate with the businesses to meet their needs of soft skill work force. As a first outcome of the engagement with the government, an MOU was signed in April 2021 between APINDO and the Ministry of Education to establish a vocational education steering forum at the central and regional levels and to set up working groups for industrial partners and schools. APINDO formulated advocacy strategies to participate in the decision making process of soft skills development policies and set up the plan for In Business soft skills training for the employees of member companies. The ILO supported APINDO to conduct a research on the assessment of changes in the business environment, especially in relation to needs and challenges of soft skills from the perspective of employers and the development of policy proposals for implementation of Industry 4.0 strategies. The ILO helped define the research scope and built capacity of the APINDO research institute to join the research project and develop policy positions in partnership with the external research institution.
The ILO provided technical advice to APINDO on the role of employer&#xbf;fs organization in the governance of vocational training and skills systems to ensure appropriate response to the changing business environment and address employer concerns and questions.</narrative>
   </description>
